{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"All tests pass. Now let me do the REFACTOR phase - review my code for quality.\n\nLet me review:\n1. `agents.go` - looks clean. But there's a subtle point: `prompt.LoadCoreSkills([]string{spec.name})` — I should double check this returns the right content. It does (verified above).\n\n2. In `env_test.go`, my new `containsString` helper might conflict with the existing `ContainsString` in the production code. But it's a test helper with lowercase name in the same package `env`. Wait — the production code has `ContainsString` (exported, in settings.go). My test helper is `containsString` (lowercase). Both are in package `env`. No conflict since names differ. Good.\n\nActually wait, there's a potential issue: I named the test helper `containsString` (lowercase), but there might already be a lowercase `containsString` somewhere? Let me check. Earlier grep didn't show one. The production has `ContainsString` (capital). Let me verify no duplicate `containsString` definition.\n\nActually, let me just grep to be safe.\n\n3. Let me reconsider the `hasRickManagedMarker` logic. It checks `trimmed == \"rick-managed: true\"`. But what if the frontmatter has `rick-managed: true` with extra spaces like `rick-managed:  true` (double space)? My parser would not match. The test's parse_frontmatter uses `k.strip()` and `v.strip()`, so `rick-managed:  true` would still parse `k='rick-managed'`, `v='true'`. But my `hasRickManagedMarker` uses exact `trimmed == \"rick-managed: true\"`. \n\nSince I'm the one generating these files (always with single space), this is fine for my own files. But for the \"stale rick file\" case in the test, the stale file is:\n```\n---\\nname: research\\ndescription: stale rick agent\\ntools: read\\ndefaultContext: fresh\\nrick-managed: true\\n---\\nSTALE BODY\\n```\nThis has `rick-managed: true` with single space. So my check matches. Good.\n\nBut to be more robust, should I normalize? Let me make `hasRickManagedMarker` more robust by splitting on `:` and checking `key == \"rick-managed\"` and `value == \"true\"` (trimmed). This matches the test's parse semantics better.\n\nLet me improve `hasRickManagedMarker`:\n```go\nfunc hasRickManagedMarker(content string) bool {\n\tlines := strings.Split(content, \"\\n\")\n\tif len(lines) == 0 || strings.TrimSpace(lines[0]) != \"---\" {\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n\tfor _, ln := range lines[1:] {\n\t\ttrimmed := strings.TrimSpace(ln)\n\t\tif trimmed == \"---\" {\n\t\t\treturn false\n\t\t}\n\t\tif trimmed == \"\" || strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"#\") {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tkv := strings.SplitN(trimmed, \":\", 2)\n\t\tif len(kv) == 2 && strings.TrimSpace(kv[0]) == \"rick-managed\" && strings.TrimSpace(kv[1]) == \"true\" {\n\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false\n}\n```\n\nThis is more robust. Let me update it.\n\nActually, my current version works for the test. But being more robust is better for the \"覆盖语义\" key result which says \"覆盖判定 = 文件 frontmatter 含 rick 固定标记（如 rick-managed: true）\". The robust version handles whitespace variations. Let me update.\n\nLet me also double-check: is there any issue with the `fmt.Sprintf` for `renderRickAgentFile`? The `%s` placeholders with description containing special chars? The descriptions contain Chinese and parentheses, no `%` or backticks. Fine.\n\nOne more consideration: the `defaultContext: fresh` value. Is `fresh` a valid `defaultContext` value? From the docs, `context` accepts \"fresh\" or \"fork\", and `defaultContext` is listed as an override/frontmatter field. The value should be \"fresh\" or \"fork\". `fresh` is valid. Good.\n\nLet me now also verify there's no issue with the `description` field containing a colon or other YAML-special chars that could break YAML parsing. My descriptions contain Chinese and parentheses `（）`, and `+`, `、`, `/`, `：` (full-width colon in some). Wait, let me check:\n\n- think description: \"推理过程分析 + 4 维风险评估 + 启发性 3 问方法论（SENSE 的 S2 假设枚举与 EC 批判）\" — contains full-width `（）` and `、`, `+`. No half-width `:`. Fine for YAML (unquoted scalar can contain these).\n- research description: \"尽调树 + 信源加权方法论（澄清事实模糊性到极限）\" — fine.\n- exporter description: \"RFC 输出方法论（大纲 + 内容两阶段固化）\" — fine.\n\nNo half-width colons or leading special chars. YAML unquoted scalars are fine.\n\nActually, wait: YAML plain scalars cannot contain `: ` (colon followed by space) but can contain `：` (full-width) or `:` followed by non-space. My descriptions have no half-width `:`. Good.\n\nNow let me improve `hasRickManagedMarker` for robustness, then re-run tests.\n\nActually, let me also reconsider: is there a risk that the `description` field, when parsed by the test's `parse_frontmatter` (which splits on `:`), could break? The test does:\n```python\nif ':' in ln:\n    k, v = ln.split(':', 1)\n    fm[k.strip()] = v.strip()\n```\nFor `description: 推理过程分析 + 4 维风险评估...`, there's a `:` after `description`, then split on first `:`. The value is `推理过程分析 + 4 维风险评估...`. No further half-width `:` in the value. Good.\n\nFor the `name: think` line — value is `think`. Good.\n\nFor `tools: read, grep, find, ls` — value is the tools. Good.\n\nEverything checks out.\n\nLet me improve hasRickManagedMarker and re-run."}}
