oing_check's behavior, so the current bin/rick is fine.\n\nActually, wait — let me reconsider. Should I rebuild bin/rick to include my changes? The doing_check command is in `internal/cmd/tools_doing_check.go`, which I didn't modify. So the current binary is fine for doing_check.\n\nBut there's a subtle point: the task says \"第一步：执行 Doing Loop ... COMMIT ... 运行 check 命令（使用 prompt 上下文中的 rick_bin_path 和 job_id）\". The rick_bin_path would be `bin/rick` (the local binary). Let me check what the prompt context's rick_bin_path is. In the task prompt, I don't see an explicit rick_bin_path variable, but the \"第二步：格式检查\" says `/workdir/sunquan20/AI_CODING/rick/bin/rick tools doing_check job_35`.\n\nSo I should run `/workdir/sunquan20/AI_CODING/rick/bin/rick tools doing_check job_35`.\n\nLet me now decide the commit scope. I'll commit:\n1. `internal/env/agents.go` (new file)\n2. `internal/env/check.go`\n3. `internal/env/customizations.go`\n4. `internal/env/env_test.go`\n\nThen mark task9 success, commit tasks.json separately.\n\nBut wait — should I also rebuild bin/rick and commit it? Let me check the previous feat commits more carefully. task6's feat commit c9d7470 has 14 files, no bin/rick. So the convention for these doing tasks is: source only, no binary.\n\nActually, but the binary `bin/rick` is currently modified in the working tree (dirty). If I don't commit it, it stays dirty. That's fine — it was dirty before I started, and other tasks (task7 etc.) will handle it. Actually, wait, let me reconsider — is the dirty `bin/rick` going to cause a problem with my commit? No. `git add` specific files only stages those files.\n\nLet me now also reconsider: do I need to worry about the other modified files (.rick/dream/prompts/*, .rick/loops/*, .rick/domain/*)? These are pre-existing dirty state. I'll leave them alone.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me check whether these files are actually relevant. `.rick/loops/tdd-red-green-refactor-loop.md` modified — my task references the tdd 