ing at git log, task6's feat commit is `c9d7470` and then chore `319e843`. The `bin/rick` currently modified in working tree suggests it was rebuilt AFTER the last commit (319e843) — probably as part of task9's test generation phase (the testgen prompt may have built the binary).\n\nLet me check: is `bin/rick` in .gitignore? The env.md says \"项目内 `bin/rick`（仓库跟踪二进制，gitignore 但 -f 提交）\". So `bin/rick` is tracked (force-added despite gitignore).\n\nLet me check the diff of the other files to understand what's going on, then decide my commit scope.\n\nActually, the cleanest approach: commit ONLY my task9 code files + rebuild bin/rick + commit. The other modified files (.rick/dream/prompts/*, .rick/loops/*, .rick/domain/*) are pre-existing noise. I'll leave them uncommitted (they're not my concern for task9).\n\nBut wait — will leaving them uncommitted cause issues with doing_check? doing_check only reads tasks.json and debug/. The other files don't matter.\n\nHowever, there's a concern: `bin/rick` is tracked. If I rebuild it with my changes, it will differ from HEAD. For the feat commit, should I include the rebuilt binary? Let me check what previous feat commits did — did they include bin/rick?\n\nLet me check the git history for bin/rick.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_L6CTPSDaDjxD6KSfmvbb0271","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /workdir/sunquan20/AI_CODING/rick && git log --oneline -20 -- bin/rick && echo \"=== check .gitignore ===\" && cat .gitignore"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_01_6ZFNxN5DeG2jpvPbVixJ2901","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /workdir/sunquan20/AI_CODING/rick && git show --stat c9d7470 | head -40 && echo \"=== 319e843 ===\" && git show --stat 319e843 | head -20"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"deepseek","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","usage":{"input":2257,"output":1258,"cacheRead":125184,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1092,"totalTokens":128699,"cost":{"input":0.000981795,"output":0.0010944